# SnackPortal2 frontend — environment TEMPLATE (FENV-B). NAMES ONLY, NEVER VALUES.
#
# Copy this file to `.env.local` and fill it in locally. `.env.local` is git-ignored (`*.local`),
# is loaded by Vite's `loadEnv(mode, cwd, "VITE_")`, and OVERRIDES `.env` for the same key. The
# previously tracked `.env` has been untracked under FENV-B: a tracked env file is a standing
# invitation to commit a value, and this repository already carried live Supabase configuration in
# one.
#
# ⚠️ ORDERING — this matters more than it looks. Create and populate `.env.local` BEFORE running
# anything that rewrites the working tree. Which commands can destroy an env file depends on whether
# that file is tracked, and the two hazard sets are DISJOINT:
#   - TRACKED with uncommitted edits: `git checkout -- .env`, `git stash` and `git reset --hard`
#     discard the edits; `git clean` cannot touch it — it removes untracked files only.
#   - UNTRACKED and ignored (what `.env` becomes in this clone, and what `.env.local` always is):
#     `git clean -x` removes it; the three commands above do not.
# Make the copy first and the ordering stops mattering.
#
# ⚠️ WHERE THE STANDING OIDC VALUES ACTUALLY ARE — they are NOT in this clone. The `.env` beside this
# template is a clean checkout of the base commit and carries ZERO `VITE_SP2_OIDC_*` keys. The live
# values live in the working-tree `.env` of the clone the standing frontend is actually run from. In
# THAT clone the file is TRACKED and MODIFIED — not untracked — and its committed version likewise
# carries zero `VITE_SP2_OIDC_*` keys, so the uncommitted working-tree change is the ONLY copy of
# them. `git clean` therefore cannot delete it; `git checkout -- .env`, `git stash` and
# `git reset --hard` each do. And once this removal lands, updating that clone raises a modify/delete
# conflict on exactly this file — resolved the wrong way, the values are gone.
#
# Required operator step, BEFORE this removal is committed or merged: in THAT clone, copy its `.env`
# to an ignored `.env.local` (`cp .env .env.local` — a byte copy that reads nothing out) and confirm
# the app still loads its configuration. Do not read, echo, print, or transcribe those values
# anywhere, including into this file. Following this template literally without that step produces a
# `.env.local` with no OIDC values, and the failure mode is the silent CORS block described below.
#
# ⚠️ NO SECRETS HERE, EVER. This file is tracked. Only names and shapes.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Supabase (INTERIM data layer) --
# The interim Supabase data layer is still active: `src/integrations/supabase/client.ts` THROWS if
# either of the two below is missing, and hundreds of direct-SDK call sites remain. They are
# required until the Gateway cutover completes. Both are PUBLIC client values — the publishable
# (anon) key, never a service-role key.
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=
VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# Server-side equivalents, read by the SSR/server surface.
SUPABASE_URL=
SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# ⚠️ SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY is deliberately NOT listed. Server-side code reads it, but naming an
# admin credential in a tracked template is how one ends up pasted into a tracked file. Same for
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY, DATABASE_URL, LOVABLE_API_KEY, FIRECRAWL_API_KEY and SCREENSHOT_API_KEY:
# operator-local only, and never in this repository.

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- Workspace behaviour --
VITE_WORKSPACE_ENFORCEMENT=

# Read at `src/lib/default-intake/index.ts`. It was absent from the previously tracked `.env`, so
# the app has been running on its code default — listed here so that is a decision, not an accident.
VITE_DEFAULT_INTAKE_MODE=

# ------------------------------------------------------ SnackPortal2 backend + OIDC (controlled MVP) --
# THE BACKEND IS NOW THE FastAPI BFF (D-46), not the retired API Gateway. The variable keeps its
# name to avoid churn across 37 files, but what it addresses has changed: the BFF is the single
# frontend-facing ingress, its operation surface is enumerated by contract, and there is no
# Gateway in the target runtime. The three operations this client calls — GET /memberships and
# GET|PATCH /tenant/startups/{ref} — are unchanged paths on the BFF, with the same
# `Authorization: Bearer` and `X-Tenant-Id` headers.
#
# LOCAL DEVELOPMENT (SnackPortal2 Stage 6): the BFF is published on 127.0.0.1:8000, and
# vite.config.ts proxies `/sp2-api` to it. Set this to the DEV SERVER's own origin plus that
# prefix, so the call is SAME-ORIGIN and no CORS policy on the public ingress is involved:
#
#     VITE_SP2_GATEWAY_BASE_URL=http://localhost:5173/sp2-api
#
# It must stay an ABSOLUTE http(s) URL — resolveSp2BootstrapPosture() parses it with
# parseAbsoluteHttpUrl, and a bare path resolves to fail-closed.
#
# Unset selects the dev mock adapter. See docs/Local_Development_Runbook.md in the SnackPortal2
# backend repository for the full local procedure.
VITE_SP2_GATEWAY_BASE_URL=

# The four OIDC values must agree BYTE-FOR-BYTE with the Keycloak client registration. A
# one-character mismatch is refused by the identity provider at the authorization step, or — where
# a cross-origin call is involved — produces a SILENT browser-side CORS block rather than a server
# error: the preflight OPTIONS is answered 204, the browser then never sends the real request, and
# nothing appears in any server log.
#
# ALL FOUR OR NONE. `resolveSp2BootstrapPosture()` has exactly three outcomes: `real` (base URL AND
# issuer/clientId/redirectUri all present and valid), `dev_mock` (EVERY real-integration variable
# absent, and not production), and `fail_closed` for anything in between. Partial configuration is
# forbidden, deliberately — mixed mock/real configuration is what lets a mock answer a request
# someone believes went to the backend.
#
# LOCAL DEVELOPMENT (SnackPortal2 Stage 6A). The backend repository ships a local Keycloak in
# `infrastructure/docker/docker-compose.rebuild.yml`, with its realm imported declaratively. Start
# it with `python -m tools.local.sp2_local idp-up` from that repository's `backend/` directory, then
# use exactly these values — they match the committed realm template byte for byte:
#
#     VITE_SP2_OIDC_ISSUER=http://localhost:8090/realms/sp2-local
#     VITE_SP2_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=sp2-local-web
#     VITE_SP2_OIDC_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:5173/sp2-gateway/callback
#     VITE_SP2_OIDC_POST_LOGOUT_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:5173/sp2-gateway
#
# The `localhost` spelling matters: the realm registers `http://localhost:5173` as the client's
# redirect URI and web origin, and `127.0.0.1` is a different origin to a browser.
#
# The client is PUBLIC and has no secret — Authorization Code + PKCE S256 is REQUIRED by the client
# registration, not merely offered. Nothing secret belongs in any VITE_ variable: everything Vite
# injects ships to the browser.
#
# Sign in as `acme-agent`, `zeta-agent`, `nova-agent` or `control-operator`. Their passwords are
# generated into the backend's untracked `infrastructure/docker/.env.rebuild` by
# `sp2_local init-env`, and are never printed or committed.
VITE_SP2_OIDC_ISSUER=
VITE_SP2_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=
VITE_SP2_OIDC_REDIRECT_URI=
VITE_SP2_OIDC_POST_LOGOUT_REDIRECT_URI=

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- Retired --
# `VITE_SUPABASE_PROJECT_ID` and `SUPABASE_PROJECT_ID` were tracked in the old `.env` and are read
# by NOTHING in this repository. They are deliberately not carried forward. If something starts
# reading them, add them here in the same change.
